{"t": " gives", "lp": -0.5605684518814087, "top": [[" gives", -0.5605684518814087], [" gave", -1.1546406745910645]]}, {"t": " more", "lp": -0.013216063380241394, "top": [[" more", -0.013216063380241394], [" consistent", -5.240604400634766]]}, {"t": " predictable", "lp": -0.6465317606925964, "top": [[" predictable", -0.6465317606925964], [" consistent", -0.7794700860977173]]}, {"t": " performance", "lp": -0.904212474822998, "top": [[" performance", -0.904212474822998], [" latency", -1.1509872674942017]]}, {"t": "\n", "lp": -0.028425902128219604, "top": [["\n", -0.028425902128219604], [" or", -4.179050922393799]]}, {"t": " ", "lp": -0.0006688685389235616, "top": [[" ", -0.0006688685389235616], ["-", -9.345121383666992]]}, {"t": " -", "lp": -6.664021202595904e-05, "top": [[" -", -6.664021202595904e-05], [" \u2014", -12.134378433227539]]}, {"t": " Testing", "lp": -0.7877888679504395, "top": [[" Testing", -0.7877888679504395], [" Trou", -1.90317964553833]]}, {"t": "/", "lp": -1.9241751432418823, "top": [["/b", -1.2614649534225464], [" or", -1.6043909788131714]]}, {"t": "development", "lp": -0.3012893795967102, "top": [["development", -0.3012893795967102], ["develop", -3.48126482963562]]}, {"t": " scenarios", "lp": -1.3039246797561646, "top": [[" scenarios", -1.3039246797561646], [" where", -1.3755875825881958]]}, {"t": "\n\n", "lp": -0.12206844240427017, "top": [["\n\n", -0.12206844240427017], [" comparing", -3.2830352783203125]]}, {"t": "##", "lp": -0.11767460405826569, "top": [["##", -0.11767460405826569], ["---", -2.2299373149871826]]}, {"t": " Quick", "lp": -0.42658597230911255, "top": [[" Quick", -0.42658597230911255], [" Practical", -1.1391257047653198]]}, {"t": " Reference", "lp": -0.6494536995887756, "top": [[" Reference", -0.6494536995887756], [" Summary", -0.9105830788612366]]}, {"t": "\n\n", "lp": -0.040376197546720505, "top": [["\n\n", -0.040376197546720505], [" Table", -3.2456488609313965]]}, {"t": "|", "lp": -0.0015155230648815632, "top": [["|", -0.0015155230648815632], ["```", -6.5569610595703125]]}, {"t": " Mode", "lp": -0.00025782332522794604, "top": [[" Mode", -0.00025782332522794604], [" Value", -8.795637130737305]]}, {"t": " |", "lp": -0.0009426147444173694, "top": [[" |", -0.0009426147444173694], [" CCS", -7.384772777557373]]}, {"t": " CCS", "lp": -0.0020229401998221874, "top": [[" CCS", -0.0020229401998221874], [" Behavior", -8.311724662780762]]}, {"t": " Active", "lp": -0.3900512754917145, "top": [[" Active", -0.3900512754917145], [" Enabled", -1.9139617681503296]]}, {"t": " |", "lp": -0.1918356865644455, "top": [[" |", -0.1918356865644455], ["?", -1.7480567693710327]]}, {"t": " Work", "lp": -0.05231337994337082, "top": [[" Work", -0.05231337994337082], [" Migration", -3.7027859687805176]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how the driver handles **CCS** (Compute/Context Switch) cores\u2014those secondary EU cores on each GPU slice that handle context-switching and low-priority work. On the Xe driver you can set it via kernel module parameter:\n\n```\noptions xe ccs_mode=N\n```\n\n## The Modes\n\n**`ccs_mode=1` (default)** \u2014 *CCS enabled, dynamic migration*\n- CCS cores are active and available\n- The driver can migrate work between P-cores (performance) and CCS cores as needed\n- This is the standard, recommended setting for most users\n- Gives you the full feature set with automatic load balancing\n\n**`ccs_mode=2`** \u2014 *CCS disabled entirely*\n- All CCS cores are turned off\n- All work is forced onto P-cores only\n- Useful for debugging or when CCS cores exhibit problems\n- Generally **not** recommended for performance since you lose cores\n\n## When to Use Mode 4\n\n**`ccs_mode=4`** \u2014 *CCS enabled but work stays on P-cores (static)*\n\nThis keeps CCS cores powered/enabled but prevents the driver from migrating work onto them. Think of it as a middle ground:\n\n- **Use it when:** You want CCS cores available (for their background/context-switching duties) but you don't want compute work scheduled onto them\u2014essentially `mode=2` behavior for scheduling purposes while keeping CCS functionality active.\n- **Typical use cases:**\n  - Debugging scheduling issues where you suspect the P\u2194CCS migration logic\n  - Certain workloads where forcing everything to P-cores gives more predictable performance\n  - Testing/development scenarios\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Mode | CCS Active | Work"}